 ## Overview
-The Microflake Model is a physically-based participating medium model that represents a medium as a homogeneous cloud of microscopic, perfectly specular flakes [S1]. It solves the problem of accurately rendering anisotropic media (such as fur, hair, or certain fabrics) by deriving direction-dependent attenuation and phase functions directly from a microflake normal distribution function (NDF) [S1]. Unlike generic anisotropic phase functions which often violate Helmholtz reciprocity, the Microflake Model satisfies reciprocity by construction [S1]. It is distinct from the [Single-Scattering Atmosphere Rendering](3289-single-scattering-atmosphere-rendering.md) which typically assumes isotropic or simple Henyey-Greenstein scattering, and from the [Single Scattering Approximation](5264-single-scattering-approximation.md) which may use arbitrary phase functions without physical grounding in surface geometry.
+The Microflake Model is a physically-based participating medium model that represents a volume as a homogeneous cloud of microscopic, perfectly specular flakes [S1]. It solves the problem of rendering anisotropic media (such as fur, felt, or certain powders) by deriving direction-dependent attenuation and scattering properties directly from a statistical distribution of microflake orientations [S1]. Unlike isotropic single-scattering approximations, it produces direction-dependent phase functions and attenuation coefficients that satisfy Helmholtz's reciprocity principle by construction [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The model defines the radiative transfer properties of the medium based on the statistical orientation of its constituent microflakes.
+The model assumes the medium consists of microflakes with a normal distribution function $D(\omega_m)$ and a total surface area density $\rho$ [S1]. The microflakes are assumed to be perfectly specular and thin, reflecting light from both sides [S1]. The model requires $D(\omega)$ to be symmetric, i.e., $D(\omega) = D(-\omega)$ [S1].
 
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** Microflake Normal Distribution Function $D(\omega_m)$, density $\rho$, albedo $\alpha(\lambda)$.
-*   **Output:** Attenuation coefficient $\sigma_t(\omega)$, scattering coefficient $\sigma_s(\omega)$, and phase function $f(\omega \rightarrow \omega')$ for any incoming direction $\omega$ and outgoing direction $\omega'$.
+**Inputs:**
+*   $\omega$: Incoming light direction (unit vector).
+*   $\omega'$: Outgoing view direction (unit vector).
+*   $\lambda$: Wavelength.
+*   $D(\omega_m)$: Microflake Normal Distribution Function (NDF), defined over the hemisphere or sphere.
+*   $\rho$: Density, total area of microflake surface per unit volume ($m^{-1}$) [S1].
+*   $\alpha(\lambda)$: Albedo of the microflakes, wavelength-dependent, unitless [S1].
+
+**Outputs:**
+*   $\sigma_t(\omega)$: Attenuation coefficient ($m^{-1}$) [S1].
+*   $\sigma_s(\omega)$: Scattering coefficient ($m^{-1}$) [S1].
+*   $f(\omega \rightarrow \omega')$: Phase function ($st^{-1}$) [S1].
 
